Investigation
Task 11 — Who last modified the lure document?

File: Salary_Lockheed_Martin_job_opportunities_confidential.docx

.docx files are ZIP archives, so strings on the raw file misses metadata stored in Deflate-compressed XML parts. The last-modifier value is in docProps/core.xml:

bash
# Method 1: read the core properties part directly
unzip -p Salary_Lockheed_Martin_job_opportunities_confidential.docx docProps/core.xml \
  | grep -oP '(?<=<cp:lastModifiedBy>)[^<]+'

# Method 2: exiftool
exiftool Salary_Lockheed_Martin_job_opportunities_confidential.docx | grep "Last Modified By"

Finding: Challenger. Both methods returned the same value.

Interpretation: lastModifiedBy is populated from the Office user name on the authoring system and can be set to any value. It shows which account name last saved the file. It does not establish the author's identity, and on its own it is not evidence of deliberate forgery.

Task 12 — Which directory does 17.dotm create?

File: 17.dotm (macro-enabled Word template)

As in Task 11, strings on the raw file fails because the VBA project is inside the ZIP container. Extracting word/vbaProject.bin does not fully solve this either: the VBA source in that OLE file is stored with MS-OVBA compression, so strings sees only fragments. olevba decompresses the modules and prints the source:

bash
# Confirm the file contains a VBA project
unzip -l 17.dotm | grep -i vbaProject.bin
oleid 17.dotm

# Extract and decompress macro source, with indicator analysis
olevba --decode 17.dotm

# Focus on filesystem operations
olevba 17.dotm | grep -iE 'MkDir|CreateFolder|AppData|STARTUP'

Finding: the macro creates the directory C:\Users\<USER>\AppData\Roaming\Microsoft\Word\STARTUP, submitted in the task's <USER> placeholder format.

Interpretation: Word loads templates in its startup folder as global templates whenever it starts. Creating this directory prepares a location for a template that runs on every Word launch, which maps to ATT&CK T1137.001 (Office Template Macros).

