Threat-intelligence context

Sources checked as of 2026-11-22.

Lazarus Group (G0032): described by ATT&CK as a North Korean state-sponsored group attributed to the Reconnaissance General Bureau.
Operation Dream Job (C0022): ATT&CK records activity from September 2019 to August 2020, with Operation North Star and Operation Interception as associated names. Vendors continue to report new waves under the same name, including Check Point Research reporting through 2026, with targeting focused on the defense and aerospace sectors.
DRATzarus (S0694): a remote access tool used by Lazarus against defense and aerospace organizations since at least summer 2020. ATT&CK's DRATzarus entry does not list Office template persistence. Within this Sherlock, the .dotm behavior is attributed to the scenario's loader, not to DRATzarus itself.
Defensive takeaways

Detection

Alert on file creation in %APPDATA%\Microsoft\Word\STARTUP and on new .dotm files appearing there (for example Sysmon Event ID 11, FileCreate).
Alert on Office processes (WINWORD.EXE) creating directories or writing executable content in user profile paths.

Hardening

Block macros in files from the internet (Mark of the Web), using the Microsoft 365 default policy or Group Policy.
Enable the Attack Surface Reduction rule "Block Office applications from creating executable content".
Restrict trusted locations, and monitor the Word and Excel startup folders and the GlobalDotName registry value.
Key lessons
OOXML files are ZIP archives: inspect the parts, not the raw bytes.
VBA in vbaProject.bin is compressed a second time inside OLE. Use olevba, not strings.
Report metadata as observed values. A lastModifiedBy name is a lead, not an attribution.
Keep campaign-level ATT&CK techniques separate from techniques observed in your own evidence.
